  "strategy": "### 1. Approach and Rationale\nThe approach focuses on constructing a function with minimal worst-case overlap by leveraging known structural properties: near-periodicity and potential harmonic basis effects. Unlike gradient-descending local optimization, it uses a parametric harmonic basis representation with adaptive adjustment to explicitly shape the solution. This method controls integral constraints through a projection operator and adapts the parameterization based on gradient information. The plan focuses on:\n- Identifying critical lags where overlap is highest\n- Targeting these regions with virtual perturbations\n- Using smoothing and feature extraction to maintain feasibility\n\nThe key bottleneck addressed is escaping local optima by inspecting periodic gradients rather than relying solely on gradient descent. The method combines:\n1. Harmonic parameterization with adaptive basis dimension\n2. Gradient-based lag feature weighing\n3. Projection-based integral enforcement\n4. Inversion-inspired fine adjustments\n\nDespite risks of dispersion and numerical issues, the parametric control extracts significant jumps from standard curves.\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification\n**Representation**: Parametrize h(x) as a weighted sum of $b_{\\nu, p}(x)$ functions:\n\\begin{equation}\nh(x) = \\frac{N}{2} + \\sum_{\\nu=-N}^{N} c_{\\nu} \\cdot b_{\\nu}(x)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $N$ is adaptive dimension parameter, $N = 280$ for base resolution. $b_{\\nu}$ will implement basis functions (initially using sinc-like but with adaptive harmonics).\n\n**Constraints**:\n- $\\|h\\|_{\\infty} \\le 1$: enforced by projection\n- $\\|h\\|_1 = N/2$: enforced by projection\n- Use auxiliary projection $proj(h) = \\min_{a,b \\in [0,1]} \\|h - a\\mathbf{1}-b\\mathbf{1}\\|_2$\n\n**Objective Function**:\n$f(h) = \\max_{k=-N}^{N-1} \\int_{0}^{2} h(x) \\cdot (1 - h(x+k)) dx$\nComputation using:\n$\\hat{c} = \\text{correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}='full')$\nThen $\\max_k \\hat{c}[k]$\n\n**Gradient Specification**:\nDefine LagFilter operator $F_{k_0}(h) = [h(x)(1-h(x+k_0))](\\text{full mode}))$ for $k_0 = arg\\max_k \\hat{c}[k]$\n\nThe key innovations are:\n1. $\\nabla_k F_{k_0}(h)\\big|_{x} \\propto (1 - 2h(x+k_0) + h(x)\\delta_{k_}\\partial_k_0(.)])$\n   requiring detailed indexing but finite differences can approximate\n\n2. Projection $P_{\\text{ker}}(h) \\perp \\nabla F_{k_0}$ controls feature removal\n\n### 3. Implementation Procedure\n**i. Initialize**:\n- $h_0 = \\text{initial\\_h\\_values}$\n- $N = 280$\n- $\\alpha = 1.0, \\lambda = 0.92$\n\n**ii. Optimization Loop (residual improvement < 0.001)**:\n\n1. **Feature Extraction**:\n   - $k_0 = \\arg\\max_k \\hat{c}[k]$\n   - $\\mathbf{g} = \\nabla_{ \\|} \\hat{c}[k](x) = \\nabla_{ \\|} \\int h(x)\\cdot(1-h(x+k_0)) dx$:\n        via pinv(F_wedge.reshape((1, -1))), where F_wedge = correlate(h,\u1e23, mode='full').roll(-k_0,...)\n   - Feature weighting: $W_{\\nu} = |\\mathbf{g}[\\text{indices of basis}\\nu]|$\n\n2. **Gradient-Paced Parameter Adjustment**:\n   - $\\Delta t = \\alpha.$ (step size)\n   - $\\mathbf{s} = - \\lambda \\cdots \\text{some direction}$\n\n3. **Basis Collapse/Oscillatorization**:\n   - $\\mathbf{c}_{\\nu} \\leftarrow \\mathbf{c}_{\\nu} \\exp(-\\epsilon \\cdot W_{\\nu})$ (stability)\n\n4. **Projection**:\n   - For all $\\nu$: $b_{\\nu} = \\text{sinc}\\left(\\nu \\frac{\\pi}{N}\\right) + \\sigma \\text{noise}$ (or alternative adapted)\n\n5. **Integral Enforcement**:\n   - $\\hat{h} = (\\frac{N}{2} + \\sum_{\\nu} c_\\nu b_\\nu) \\odot \\mathbb{I}_{[0,1]}$\n   - If $\\| \\hat{h} \\|_1 \\neq \\frac{N}{2}$: scale by $\\frac{N/2}{\\|\\hat{h}\\|_1}$\n\n**iii. Convergence Check**: \nCheck for stable basis coefficients and k_0 improvement\n\n### 4. Search Choices and Variations\nThe method will be assessed at two resolutions: \n- Base resolution (280 samples)\n- Upsampled resolution (560 with periodicity preserved)\n\nFor resampling, simply double the harmonic basis parameters $(\\nu)$ while preserving derivatives.\n\nAdaptive components include:\n- $\\alpha \\leftarrow max(\\alpha \\lambda, 0.001)$ reduction\n- $\\lambda \\leftarrow \\lambda^{\\text{momentum risk indicator}}$\n- Feature retention $W_{\\nu} \\to \\theta(W_{\\nu}\\text{kl-divergence prior})$\n\n### 5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nAll operations occur in 280, 560 or dynamically varying runtime dimensions. Main computational load stems from correlation operations (O(N log N)) and matrix inverses (O(N^3)). \n\nFeasible implementation via:\n- numpy.correlate() for evaluation\n- scipy.linalg.solve() for inversion when required\n- Early termination on negligible residual decrease\n\nThe algorithm is robust against:\n- C2 type errors (projections enforce bounds)\n- d1-d5 overflow (inactive since scalar N=560)\n- Misaligned weighting (handled by \u03b7-damping)\n\n### 6. Validation and Return Contract\n**Validation**:\n- Check $\\sum_i h[i] = 280$ exactly when scaling\n- Verify $0 \\le h[i] \\le 1$ after projection\n- Re-compute C5 with brute-force overlap calculation\n\n**Output Format**:\n(h, best_c5, n)\n\nwhere:\n- h = current candidate (vector)\n- best_c5 = float of minimal C5\n- n = original resolution 280",
